# AWS CLI and System Stress Testing on Ubuntu 24.04

## Simple Step-by-Step Guide

Follow the steps in order. Run one command at a time and check the output before continuing.

---

## Step 1 — Update Ubuntu

First update the package list:

```bash
sudo apt update
```

---

## Step 2 — Install Required Packages

Install `unzip` and `curl`:

```bash
sudo apt install unzip curl -y
```

---

## Step 3 — Download AWS CLI v2

Download the AWS CLI installer:

```bash
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
```

---

## Step 4 — Extract AWS CLI

Extract the ZIP file:

```bash
unzip -q awscliv2.zip
```

---

## Step 5 — Install AWS CLI

Run the installer:

```bash
sudo ./aws/install
```

---

## Step 6 — Verify AWS CLI

Check the installed version:

```bash
aws --version
```

Open AWS CLI help if required:

```bash
aws help
```

---

## Step 7 — Install the Stress Tool

Update packages and install `stress`:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install stress -y
```

---

## Step 8 — Generate CPU Load for 10 Minutes

Run this command to use all available CPU cores for 600 seconds:

```bash
stress --cpu $(nproc) --timeout 600
```

The command will stop automatically after **600 seconds (10 minutes)**.

---

## Step 9 — Install Advanced Monitoring and Stress Tools

Install `stress-ng`, `sysstat`, `iotop` and `iftop`:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y stress-ng sysstat iotop iftop
```

Check the `stress-ng` version:

```bash
stress-ng --version
```

---

## Step 10 — Start Continuous System Load

Run:

```bash
nohup stress-ng --cpu 0 --cpu-load 70 --vm 0 --vm-bytes 60% --io 2 --hdd 2 --hdd-bytes 1G --timeout 0 > /tmp/stress-ng.out 2>&1 & echo $! > /tmp/stress.pid
```

> `--timeout 0` means the stress test continues until you stop it manually.

---

## Step 11 — Monitor CPU and Memory

Use `top`:

```bash
top
```

Check memory:

```bash
free -h
```

Check CPU and disk statistics:

```bash
iostat
```

Check disk I/O:

```bash
iotop
```

Check network traffic:

```bash
iftop
```

---

## Step 12 — Check the Stress Process ID

The process ID was saved in this file:

```bash
cat /tmp/stress.pid
```

---

## Step 13 — Stop the Continuous Stress Test

Stop normally:

```bash
kill $(cat /tmp/stress.pid)
```

If it does not stop, force stop it:

```bash
kill -9 $(cat /tmp/stress.pid)
```

---

## Step 14 — Verify That Stress Testing Stopped

Run:

```bash
ps aux | grep stress-ng
```

Check that the stress process is no longer running.

---

## Important

Do not leave continuous stress testing running unnecessarily. It uses CPU, memory and disk I/O and may increase EC2 usage/cost.

For CloudWatch practicals, start the stress test only when you need to observe monitoring metrics and stop it after testing.
